bodyText:
The Fit Premises Act gives every renter of a covered unit a list of duties. The tenant must:
- follow the local board-of-health rules that materially affect physical health and safety;
- keep the unit clean and safe and not unreasonably burden any common area;
- dispose of all garbage and other waste in a clean and safe manner;
- keep plumbing fixtures as sanitary as they permit;
- use the electrical, plumbing, sanitary, heating and other facilities and appliances reasonably;
- occupy the unit in the manner it was designed for, and not add occupants beyond the number in the lease without your written permission;
- stay current on all payments the lease requires;
- and follow every rule and requirement of the lease, including any smoking rule.

The tenant must also cooperate in maintaining the unit, and must not commit the prohibited acts.

These duties matter most as a condition: a tenant who is not meeting them is not entitled to the Act's rent abatement or repair-and-deduct remedies, and a domestic violence victim's early termination generally requires the same compliance.

They are statutory, but the eviction statute's perform-or-quit notice speaks of a condition or covenant of the lease, so put them in the lease: the library's tenant-maintenance, permitted-occupants, rent and rules clauses carry most of them, and "Additional Tenant Duties Under Utah Law" clause adds the rest. The Fit Premises Act covers a tenant's principal residence, not boarding or rooming houses, mobile home lots or recreational property rented occasionally.
